00:02:09.644 --> 00:02:12.960
So on GCP right now we
have three storage options.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.930
We have our optic
storage, and we actually

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:20.760
have two storage offerings that
are both for block storage,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.460
available for Compute Engine,
our VM, or base product.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.470
GCS is our optic storage.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.810
It's geo redundant,
primarily used

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.150
for live and archival data--

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.030
a lot of more
streaming work cases.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.580
For block storage,
or for GCE, we

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.040
have what's called local SSD.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.660
And the way to think
about local SSD,

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.410
is that it is running in
the same machine as your VM,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.760
or as the instance.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.720
It tends to be used for
workloads that require

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.890
very ultra high performance.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.500
However, the one caveat
is that it's [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.180
So, if anything should
happen to that VM,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.850
your data is also gone.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.580
So, the life of your
data exists just

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.892
in the construct
of that instance.

00:03:08.892 --> 00:03:10.350
Persistent Disk,
on the other hand,

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.850
which is the other
storage offering,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:18.360
is available for any VM that
you're using as part of GCE.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.470
It is highly durable, which
means that the disk is

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.790
a standalone entity.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.710
If the VM goes
away, or comes back,

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:30.090
or you would like to use
that disk of different VMs,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.270
you can do that.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.170
Fairly high
performance, and so this

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.330
is a product that's targeted
at enterprise applications.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.290
Any virtualized
enterprise applications

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.010
requiring virtual
lock storage is where

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.290
you would use Persistent Disk.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.917
So, when you're thinking
of performance-- that's

00:03:47.917 --> 00:03:49.500
the level of performance
we're talking

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.780
about with Persistent Disk.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.597
And just as a caveat,
this is the product

00:03:53.597 --> 00:03:54.930
we're going to talk about today.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.060
If you're interested,
there are lots of sessions

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.330
focused specifically on GCS.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.630
Local SSD is being addressed in
the context of Compute Engine.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.480
And so, if you're
interested in learning more,

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.930
I'd suggest some of the
Compute Engine sessions.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.780
And today we're talking
about Persistent Disk, which

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.480
you'll hear me refer to as PD.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.459
I do have some sessions
listed at the back

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:18.334
of the presentations.

00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:20.899
So if anyone's interested in
some of those other sessions,

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:23.760
it's in the presentation.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.330
So starting with Persistent
Disk and Two SKUs--

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:31.050
I think one of the key
things about GCP in general,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.110
and specific to GCE
and Persistent Disk,

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.050
is we really try to keep it
simple for our customers.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.600
We don't have a
large set of options

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.710
available for customers.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.760
And the idea there, is
that rather than customers

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.689
having to really
think hard about--

00:04:46.689 --> 00:04:47.730
what are their workloads?

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:48.930
What are their use cases?

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.810
And then try to map it to
different SKUs offering--

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.955
we really try to keep it
simple, as to two offerings.

00:04:54.955 --> 00:04:56.580
And you'll see a lot
of things are just

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.650
bundled as part of
the base offering,

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:05.470
or these two SKUs will stretch
and fit different workloads.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.090
So starting out with
key characteristics,

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.050
PD Standard is
really what you would

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:15.000
see being used for more of
our general purpose workloads.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.394
The thing about there-- it's
good enough performance.

00:05:17.394 --> 00:05:19.560
And when I say, good enough,
people are always like,

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.750
Grace-Ann, what is good enough?

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.600
Good enough turns
out to be good enough

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.531
for the bulk or the large swath
of enterprise applications,

00:05:27.531 --> 00:05:30.030
that really don't need ultra
high performance that you would

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.865
see, or that's available,
with your flash drives.

00:05:33.865 --> 00:05:35.490
And when I'm talking
performance there,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.580
I'm talking both
latency and also IOPs.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.100
And so, if you've got your
enterprise database that

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.890
will really need
high throughput,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.550
high bandwidth, high latency,
then definitely PD SSD

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.716
is most likely your product.

00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:51.090
The other thing
there is you tend

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.720
to be less price sensitive for
those types of applications.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.800
Those are the applications
that you would run on PD SSD.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.750
For most of your
other applications,

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.030
they do fairly well
on PD Standard.

00:06:06.846 --> 00:06:08.715
And this is just a typical--

00:06:08.715 --> 00:06:10.090
and I know that
in the enterprise

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:14.260
there really isn't any
typical applications.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.270
And I know that
people say, well, I

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.950
show enterprise apps as PD SSD.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:24.780
Truthfully, they run in
either of these columns.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.540
Other key points, as to
just give you a sense of--

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.220
how do we differentiate
these two?

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.570
What are the key characteristics
between PD Standard and PD SSD?

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.524
You can see IOPs per gig.

00:06:35.524 --> 00:06:37.440
And here you're seeing
a meaningful difference

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.060
between the two offerings.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.090
For PD SSD you've
got 30 IOPs per gig,

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.320
whereas for Standard
it's just 0.75 for read

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.530
and 1.5 for write.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.560
For things, if you
look at your max IOPs,

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.180
you're going to see for
PD Standard, for reads

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.880
it's 3k, for writes 15k.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.080
PD SSD, on the other hand--

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.572
it's 40k read IOPs,
and 30k for write IOPs.

00:07:04.572 --> 00:07:06.030
And An I'll just
pause for a minute

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:10.050
here and comment that-- that
is actually something new.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.540
Today, at Next, one of the
things we're announcing

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.640
is the higher IOPs
availability for PD SSD.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.990
That is available today.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.160
For many of you who said
that you are using PD,

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.130
if you're using
Persistent Disk SSD,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.490
you're getting that
performance level today.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:31.260
Or you should be, and if
you're not, please let us know.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.510
And last point on this,
because I know a lot of people

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.718
are probably wondering, so,
what's the big difference

00:07:35.718 --> 00:07:37.320
according to--

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.300
that goes beyond
the characteristics

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.980
of the drives themselves.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.590
You can see there is a big
significant price difference

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.380
between these two offerings.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:49.560
OK.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.050
So, just talking a
bit more about what

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.090
is Persistent Disk
and the key features.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.910
So, I mentioned earlier
that it's persistent.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.910
And that really just comes
back to this construct of,

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.500
the life of your
disk is not tied

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.050
to the life of your instance.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.150
And so, however,
most of the time

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.830
to use your Persistent
Disk, someone

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:12.900
creates a VM on Compute Engine,
and with that you're also going

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.840
to create a disk to go with it.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.509
Anything should
happen to that disk,

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:19.800
anything should happen to
the connectivity to the disk,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:20.899
the disks lives on.

00:08:20.899 --> 00:08:22.440
If you should then
decide that you're

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:25.890
going to have another
application that needs access

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:29.730
to that same data, just
create that new instance

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.669
and connect it to your disk,
and you're up and running.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.900
We have this idea of
how many disks that you

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.440
can attach to a VM, and
we hear this fairly often.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.180
And so, the number
of disks that you

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.990
can attach to any given instance
is 16 drives, or 16 disks.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:56.430
And you can have both read
write and read only disks.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:57.990
At any given time
you can only have

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:01.530
one read write disk attached
to a VM, but the number of read

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.990
only will vary.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:07.130
And that's really up
for you to decide.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:08.130
And here, you can also--

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.180
Another feature of the
product is, you can have--

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.640
for those read only disks--

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.200
you can have those being
read to from multiple VMs.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.130
Also, once again,
that's up for you

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:21.780
to decide for your
different environment.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:28.080
And on the subject
of availability,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:31.030
PD is a zonal service.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:35.100
And what that means is that
one of the only restrictions

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.750
around Persistent Disk,
is that it needs to live,

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:45.510
or it does live, in the same
zone as your VM instance.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.704
They are highly available,
and under the covers one

00:09:48.704 --> 00:09:50.370
of the things we do
with Persistent Disk

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:52.980
to drive the high
availability is

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.160
for every write there's
actually three replicas written.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.080
Customers are, of
course, only charged

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.830
for one of those copies.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.130
However, the way that we
deliver greater availability

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:07.980
to customers is this
idea of the multiple

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:11.860
writes being done
under the covers.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.650
And this here is
just intended to show

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.290
that-- this whole construct
of, if you have multiple PDs,

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:19.440
and you happen to have those
instances in different zones,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.510
the disks that attach
to those instances

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:24.330
will also exist in
those different zones.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:32.320
Talk a bit about capacity,
and that the actual numbers

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:33.520
fill the systems.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:38.377
So, the maximum PD size
right now is 64 terabytes.

00:10:38.377 --> 00:10:40.210
One of the things we
were very interested in

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:43.330
is if anyone has
the need, or how big

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:47.424
is the need for that
number to increase.

00:10:47.424 --> 00:10:49.840
It's one of those things that
we've been working on doing,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.450
but priorities change.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.360
And so, I'd love to get a signal
as to how important that is.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.180
For most customers
we're seeing that 30--

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:01.330
20 to 30 --terabytes
is good enough.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:04.090
We do have some
larger, but that's

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.670
something that we'd be
interested to know about.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:09.620
Coming back to this--

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:13.060
how many disk size to a VM.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.500
If you recall
earlier, I mentioned

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:17.350
that you can actually have
multiple disks attached

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:18.635
to a VM.

00:11:18.635 --> 00:11:20.260
One of the constraints,
even though you

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:23.790
can go up to 16 disks--

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:27.880
the sum of those 16 disks
cannot exceed 64 terabytes.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:31.810
So, you can't have
16 64 terabyte disks

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:32.880
attached to a VM.

00:11:35.475 --> 00:11:36.850
And I think one
of the key things

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:41.500
here is this whole construct of,
do you need to stripe or not.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.190
And this is one of
the things that's

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:48.430
a key differentiator for us,
is this idea that you can go up

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:54.370
to, have this very large
drive and not have to stripe.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:57.610
So, there's a whole level
of management complexity,

00:11:57.610 --> 00:11:59.620
or management overhead,
that you don't even

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:02.530
need to think about as you scale
your data set to these larger

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:05.200
numbers.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.740
And then, lastly, I think
one of the other things

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:12.250
that the team
stresses with me is

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:15.430
this whole construct of being
able to hot grow your disk.

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:20.390
So, often you're not sure
what size disk to get.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:26.920
So, you start small,
and as your needs

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.430
grow you can readily
just grow your disk

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:34.990
size without having to stop
and restart, or do anything

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.850
on the VM.

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:40.060
So, once again, just giving
you a lot of flexibility

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.461
without any downtime
in your environment.

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.240
On the subject of
encryption and security--

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:49.700
and this is one
of those features

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.950
we're coming back to
the point of trying

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:53.510
to keep the product
simple, trying

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.750
not to have a whole
bunch of bells

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:59.150
and whistles that people
have to think about

00:12:59.150 --> 00:13:03.000
for their different data
sets in their environment.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:09.200
With Persistent Disk, and
on the platform as a whole,

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:10.730
all of your data is encrypted.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:13.730
And it's always on.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:15.170
It's one of the core features.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:19.940
And we support both--

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:23.190
by default it's
Google generated keys.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.970
But for instances or customers
where they have their own keys,

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:27.030
we also support that.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:34.460
So, it's fully integrated
with our Cloud Key MS System.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:37.200
And I think one of
the other things

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.400
on the subject of security is--

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:45.440
because Google Cloud runs
entirely in the Google Network,

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.370
your data never leaves
the Google Network.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:50.150
So, there's no concern about
being on the public internet.

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:51.710
And so, there's
this idea of, well,

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:55.160
what about data in flight.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.356
And so, the question
is, well, it's

00:13:57.356 --> 00:13:58.730
never leaving the
Google Network,

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:00.438
and it's encrypted
whilst on the network.

00:14:00.438 --> 00:14:03.440
So, it's just a way
of-- a different way

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:06.860
of thinking of the product,
and of security, and the data

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:10.040
on the network.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:10.804
OK.

00:14:10.804 --> 00:14:13.580
ANTON SUKHANOV: Performance,
we take very seriously

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:14.610
on Persistent Disk.

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:17.150
And today, we are
proud to announce

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:21.050
substantial performance
increases for our PD SSD

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:21.890
product.

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:28.340
For IOPs we increased
from 25,000 to 30,000 IOPs

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:32.120
on writes, and 40,000 read IOPs.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:35.900
And throughput increases
are even more substantial.

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:39.050
We go from 240
megabytes per second,

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:42.140
to 400 megabytes
per second writes,

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.950
and 800 megabytes
per second reads.

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:55.010
Also, today we are announcing a
full duplex on SSD throughput.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:56.280
What does it mean?

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:00.830
It means that you can achieve
400 megabyte per second reads,

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:04.940
and 800 megabyte per second
writes at the same time.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:09.050
This is not true for IOPs.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:14.070
So, IOPs you still can do
either one or another limits.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:17.360
But for throughput,
duplex throughput

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.900
is important for many
enterprise applications,

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:24.260
and we were asked many times
to provide this feature.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:28.460
So, it's really
exciting to date.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:33.380
Now, I want to mention that
when we talk about IOPs,

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:36.690
it's important to understand
that Persistent Disk is

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:41.780
a networking disk, which is
really cool for availability.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.655
Because you can take
one Persistent Disk,

00:15:43.655 --> 00:15:48.050
as Grace-Ann mentioned,
and attach it to any VM.

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:49.580
And you can shut down the VM.

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:52.710
You can keep your disks
and attach them later.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:56.120
It also brings high availability
of the Persistent Disks.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.700
But it comes with some
cost, and the cost

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.220
is a little bit
of extra latency.

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:05.270
Our latencies are
competitive on the market.

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:08.770
But we need to understand that
to get to the high IOPs limit,

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:14.600
your workloads should
maintain certain queue depths,

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.990
especially for these
ultra high IOPs loads.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:27.200
The new increases, they
come for large VMs.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:32.240
So, for VMs up to 15 cores
we keep the old limits.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:35.880
But from 16 cores and up
the limits are increased,

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:41.150
and the maximum limits can be
achieved on 32 and higher VMs--

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:42.040
32 cores and higher.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:51.500
Persistant Disk
Standard, at this point,

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:52.940
characteristics are not changed.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:55.400
But I just would
like to mention again

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:59.180
that we offer a very competitive
price for performance

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:01.570
ratio for PD Standard Disc.

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:05.930
Two And currently it can
go to 15,000 write IOPs,

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:09.800
and up to 3,000 read IOPs.

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:13.079
And throughput is 120
megabytes per second on writes,

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:14.339
and 180 on reads.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:22.180
So a little bit about how
we approach performance.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:25.849
For us, the simplicity is
very important in our approach

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:28.830
to both SKUs and performance.

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:32.490
We want to offer
consistent performance.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:36.090
So, you pay a certain
price for size.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.200
You get IOPs.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:38.970
You get throughput
with the size.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:44.830
And you get consistent
performance in every zone

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:48.720
where Persistent
Disk is offered.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:50.970
You don't need to
bother with striping,

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:52.140
as Grace-Ann mentioned.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:59.280
And in Persistent Disk, to
get the best performance,

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:03.510
you can just attach one large
disk if it suits your workload.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:07.770
Or, another choice, is you
can attach many small disks,

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:10.110
and you can attach
them to the same VM,

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.680
and you get the same
performance as large disks.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.270
We know that some
other solutions they

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:22.590
require striping, which is
not a very good solution

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:23.440
for the customers.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:29.250
So, we are happy that we
offer simplicity here.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:34.140
The disk size-- we want you
to have the disk size chosen

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:37.405
for performance and for space.

00:18:37.405 --> 00:18:39.280
And then if you need to
get more performance,

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:41.460
we have online any size for you.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:49.720
And by the way,
performance is something

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.480
that we take really
seriously in PD,

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:54.970
and we are always
looking for feedback.

00:18:54.970 --> 00:18:57.580
I'm sure we have
customers in the room,

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:00.400
and we would like to learn
more about your scenarios.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:04.070
And If you think we need
to deliver more IOPs,

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:06.800
more throughput--

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:10.420
if you think our model is too
simple, We would like to talk.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.700
GRACE-ANN BAKER: OK.

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:16.750
Snapshots.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.325
And so, one of the
big requirements

00:19:19.325 --> 00:19:21.450
we hear from enterprises,
or most of our customers,

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.130
is, so, what happens in
the event of failure?

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:27.100
Or, what if I want
multiple copies of my data?

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.170
How do you do that?

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:31.600
What do you have
available for us?

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:34.250
First thing we
have is snapshots.

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:35.200
And think of this.

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.510
This is very similar
to what most of you

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:41.230
are familiar with a non
param storage array.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:43.940
Snapshots exist
independent of the source.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:47.200
And what I mean by
that, is that snapshots

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:50.800
will exist in a
different zone and region

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.871
than the actual primary data.

00:19:52.871 --> 00:19:55.120
And let me just pause for a
second about the construct

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.100
of a zone and region.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:01.060
GCP is actually set up,
and it's broken down

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:03.880
into-- you have your geo areas.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:05.680
Nested inside of
the geo areas you

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:06.970
have your different regions.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.380
And then inside of the
region you have your zone--

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:11.680
similar to other cloud products.

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:14.680
I don't think anyone has
really filed a patent on how

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:18.070
they structure-- that overall
structure, or tearing,

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:19.797
of the network.

00:20:19.797 --> 00:20:21.380
For snapshots, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:22.990
are for Persistent Disk.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:26.830
You have your VM, and the
data associated with the VM

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:29.305
is sitting in a zone.

00:20:29.305 --> 00:20:32.170
And here I talk about zone
A, just to make it simple.

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:34.870
And zone A, and the
VM, and the disk,

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:38.200
are all sitting in
region A. The top, what

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:41.380
it's trying to show here,
is that you have a snapshot.

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:45.340
And that snapshot is
actually living elsewhere

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:48.780
in a different region
than your primary data.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.740
Key thing here, and
the key ask that we

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:55.210
get from customers is well, what
happens if zone A disappears?

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:56.800
We've all been
around for a while,

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.260
a zone isn't likely to
just, poof, and disappear.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:01.990
But what if there's a
failure in that zone?

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.120
What if I lose that data?

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:06.520
Will I also lose the
copy of that data?

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:07.330
The answer is, no.

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:09.941
Under most circumstances
you shouldn't.

00:21:09.941 --> 00:21:12.440
Because it's quite unlikely
that you're going to have a fail

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:17.290
that will affect both region
a and a region somewhere else

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:17.950
in the country.

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:24.340
One of the key things about
snapshots is, today, right now,

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:26.250
snapshots, it's totally--

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:29.522
it's a feature that's available
as part of Persistent Disk,

00:21:29.522 --> 00:21:31.480
but the control is totally
within the customer.

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:34.070
So, the frequency--
well, first and foremost,

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:36.170
--if you'd like to
snapshot your data,

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.680
that's something that we
leave up to customers.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:41.570
And not all customers choose
to snapshot their data.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:45.100
So, let me just start with that.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:47.500
And then the next thing is,
the frequency of snapshotting

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:49.990
is also something
that's up to customers.

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:52.390
The technology right
now is not intended

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:55.070
to be used as an alternative
to synchronous replication.

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:59.260
So, you know, the idea of
snapshotting every minute,

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:01.649
or every couple of minutes--

00:22:01.649 --> 00:22:03.940
the system wasn't built for
that, and what will happen,

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:06.356
quite frankly, is that you'll
see that your snapshots take

00:22:06.356 --> 00:22:08.650
a very long time to complete.

00:22:08.650 --> 00:22:14.620
However, what we do see,
and where the system

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:17.530
is more optimized for
is snapshots daily,

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:21.740
snapshots every couple of
hours average during a day.

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:24.880
Those are the typical use cases
that we see for snapshots.

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:27.060
And they are primarily what
people are using it for,

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:30.100
is to have a backup copy, or
have a second or third copy

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:31.150
of a given data set.

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:36.520
Other big question
we often get about

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:38.380
is, where my snapshot stored?

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:42.140
And I know there's some people
that says, why does it matter?

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:44.140
And I know for customers
that the big thing here

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:47.560
is, coming back to this question
of, in the event of a failure,

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:51.520
does it have the same
risk as the active data?

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:54.520
But some customers just want
to know that regionally-- there

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:57.640
are some geopolitical issues
at play for some customers.

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:01.700
And so, the answer here is,
just it's stored in GCS.

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:04.990
Right now we do not
make any of the GCS APIs

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:06.790
available for the objects.

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:08.470
And so, the only
access that customers

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:11.620
have to these snapshots
is via Persistent Disk.

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:14.530
But if there are
customers that are

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:16.300
interested in that
level of functionality,

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:20.050
please ping me after, because I
would love to better understand

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:20.770
that use case.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:26.890
And one of the key
things about snapshots

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:29.800
and how they are used,
is this idea of-- you

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:31.660
can restore a snapshot anywhere.

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:33.900
And so quite often, what
we see customers doing--

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:35.840
and just earlier today
we had a customer

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.740
who was using Persistent Disk.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:42.250
And in region A--

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:43.060
excuse me.

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:47.260
And they now would
like to fire up

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:52.066
a different database instance
using the same data set.

00:23:52.066 --> 00:23:55.360
They're in the
midwest, and they have

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:57.160
an office in the west
coast, and they'd

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:58.870
like to use that same data set.

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:01.690
And so, one of the great
use cases for snapshots,

00:24:01.690 --> 00:24:05.440
is you take a snapshot
of the data set,

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:09.130
you create an instance
in that second zone,

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:12.190
create your Persistent
Disk in that second zone,

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:15.250
and at the same time, then copy
the data from your snapshot

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:19.390
over to that second location.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:23.140
And that's one way of getting
access to that same data set

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:25.960
in multiple locations.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.180
We have-- the
other use case here

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:31.632
is a temporal one, where
you have Persistent Disk

00:24:31.632 --> 00:24:32.840
and you've got all your data.

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:35.230
You're using it this
week, and it's data

00:24:35.230 --> 00:24:38.050
that you only use once a month.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:40.570
Rather than pay for the disk
and keep it live all the time,

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:42.070
they'll take a
snapshot of the data.

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:43.000
Park it.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.330
And a month from now they'll
create another instance,

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:49.100
remount the snapshot,
and use the data again.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:53.140
So it's just another
way of having access

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.800
to your data in a more
temporal fashion than having

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:57.820
it mounted all the time.

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:03.480
One of the key things
about snapshots

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:06.030
is this idea that
it's cost efficient.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:09.390
This becomes relevant,
because customers

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:12.630
are very cognizant of
the side of these--

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:16.410
you're keeping
copies of your data.

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:18.450
How much data are you
keeping, and a lot of it

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:19.900
is repetitive data.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:22.260
And so, do you really need
to keep many, many copies

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:23.650
of the same data?

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:30.180
So, our snapshots are cost
efficient in the sense of,

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:33.630
we do both full and
incremental snapshots.

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:36.420
Generally the first time
that you take a snapshot,

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:39.540
we will do a snapshot
of the entire disk,

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:41.760
and that's considered
a full snapshot.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:45.270
For future snapshots we
will take differentials.

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:47.250
And so, only the blocks
that have changed

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:51.750
will be kept on a
long term basis.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.000
There are times in the
future-- so it's not just

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.400
one full and incremental
indefinitely.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:00.570
We do periodically do full also.

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:02.490
The key point is
it's not-- if you

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:04.740
chose to snapshot
on a daily basis,

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.610
we're not doing full
snapshots every day.

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:13.740
ANTON SUKHANOV: Some
of the best practices

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:16.920
for performance at snapshots.

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:20.640
So, we think it's a good
practice to separate data

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:24.450
logs and the boot disks.

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:28.890
It makes it easier for you to
managed backups and restores.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:30.886
Don't worry if your
boot disk is small.

00:26:30.886 --> 00:26:32.760
And we know some customers,
they tell us they

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:35.470
create big disks for
boot and for data,

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:38.220
because bigger disks
have better performance.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:45.000
We optimize even small
disks for good performance.

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.110
So, the striping we
mentioned, sizing

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:51.600
for space and performance,
resize, the queue depths.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:56.070
We also recommend,
if it is possible,

00:26:56.070 --> 00:27:00.810
to adjust to your workload to
have a more localized access.

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:03.180
You will get somewhat
better performance

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:06.440
from Persistent Disk.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:10.320
For snapshots, you
can take snapshots

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:11.892
without stopping your workload.

00:27:11.892 --> 00:27:13.350
I think it's a
really cool feature,

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:17.670
and it actually has no
effect on the workload.

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:19.680
So, you can keep
writing to the disk,

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:21.460
take snapshot at the same time.

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:25.440
As a matter of fact,
take multiple snapshots.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:31.110
But you have to understand
that your application should

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:33.360
be able to restore
data at the point

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:34.630
where you take a snapshot.

00:27:34.630 --> 00:27:37.860
Most databases and
file systems they can,

00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:39.720
but it's something
to be aware of.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:44.700
Test restore operations.

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:49.410
As we like to say,
good engineers backup,

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:51.510
and awesome engineers restore.

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:55.380
So, back up your data, restore
periodically in different zones

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:58.890
to make sure everything works.

00:27:58.890 --> 00:28:04.350
About the schedule,
or snapshot schedule,

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:09.420
first of all making snapshots
every second may be--

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:13.170
it's possible, but it's
not something we recommend.

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:17.100
Half an hour, every half
an hour, every one hour,

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:20.100
is acceptable-- maybe every day.

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:26.490
We have customers who have
custom scripts for their needs.

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:37.980
Try not to schedule snapshots
of business or week days.

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:40.320
And the important
for snapshots--

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:43.940
because we do snapshots
incrementally,

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:46.890
your next snapshots
will be fast.

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:50.940
It can be-- most snapshots are
actually done within minutes.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:56.160
You can make it even faster and
reduce cost if you use stream,

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:58.350
if it's not enabled.

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:02.760
Because we do not snapshot
data which is not on the disk.

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:09.990
GRACE-ANN BAKER: And then GKE.

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:12.280
This is Kubernetes,
our Container Engine.

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:14.820
I think the key point here--

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.090
I am not a GKE expert,
so let me start there.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:20.490
The key point here is that for--

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:23.940
Kubernetes actually
uses-- or GKE

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:28.830
actually uses Persistent Disk as
the storage for the containers.

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:32.460
So, when you create a container,
Kubernetes cluster, the storage

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:35.940
or persistent volumes that are
made available to the cluster

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:40.030
are all Persistent Disks,
running under the cover--

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:41.281
is working with the GKE team.

00:29:41.281 --> 00:29:42.780
They wanted me to
mention this here.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:46.602
And if there's any questions,
or interest in this,

00:29:46.602 --> 00:29:48.060
there are-- as I
mentioned earlier,

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:49.195
there's other sessions.

00:29:49.195 --> 00:29:50.820
Or, I think there's
folks here that can

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.740
answer any of those questions.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:56.690
